# require.config — `M154g` step 4a: `C95` (`config:directive:require`).
#
# Two required variables, and only one of them is referenced anywhere — `C95_TOKEN` reaches a
# header, `C95_UNUSED` reaches nothing. That asymmetry is the row's sharpest claim: the directive
# is a precondition on the **environment**, not a check on use sites, so a run with `C95_TOKEN`
# set is still refused and the refusal names `C95_UNUSED` alone.
#
# ## `M156f.2` — the sentence above was false for as long as this fixture existed
#
# The header was written `is "{env(C95_TOKEN)}"`, and that reaches **nothing**. tflw has `{var}`
# interpolation and it has `env(NAME)` as a value; the braced spelling is neither, and ships the
# literal sixteen characters over the wire. So the plant's own setup did not do what its own
# comment said, and the asymmetry it exists to demonstrate had *two* unreferenced variables rather
# than one — while checking green and reading correctly.
#
# It was not found by anyone reading it. It was found by tflw growing `TF078` (`D778`) and pointing
# at it, which is the argument for the code in one line.
#
# **The repair is the working spelling, not the braced one kept deliberately.** Both were open:
# `M156e` needed a `TF078` fixture and this was a real instance of it. But `C95`'s claim *is* the
# asymmetry, and a braced header leaves nothing referenced, so keeping it here would have preserved
# the diagnostic at the cost of the plant. `TF078` gets its own fixture in `CONFIG_FIXTURES`
# instead — one fixture, one claim.
#
# `api` points at port 9 — on the fetch standard's blocked-ports list — so the leg that gets *past*
# the gate fails with "no socket was opened" whether or not apiV2 is up. The row needs to tell
# "refused before the run started" from "ran and failed", and a port that can never answer is the
# only way to make that distinction hold on a busy box and on an idle one.

defaults
  header "X-C95" is env(C95_TOKEN)

env local default
  api "http://127.0.0.1:9/v1"

require env C95_TOKEN, C95_UNUSED
